## Introduction: What the Web Can Do That Print Can't

A newspaper can inform you. A radio broadcast can move you. A television report can show you. But none of them can listen to you.

The web can. It's the only mass medium where the audience can do something and get something back. Click a button. Type an answer. Drag a slider. The page responds. That basic capability — a reader does something, the page changes — is what makes interactive journalism possible.

And newsrooms have been exploiting it for years.

The New York Times' [dialect quiz](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2014/upshot/dialect-quiz-map.html) asks you a series of questions about how you talk — do you say "sneakers" or "tennis shoes"? — and generates a personalized heat map that pinpoints where you grew up. It became the single most-visited piece of content on the Times' website the year it was published, attracting tens of millions of visitors.

