/>

The Times' Upshot section built a [rent vs. buy calculator](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2014/upshot/buy-rent-calculator.html) that takes your income, savings, and local housing prices and tells you whether you're better off renting or buying. Their ["You Draw It"](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2017/01/15/us/politics/you-draw-obama-legacy.html) series asks readers to sketch their guess on a chart — how did the economy change under Obama? — then reveals the real data on top of your drawing.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-4/nyt-you-draw-it.png"
  alt="The New York Times 'You Draw It' interactive where readers draw their prediction on a chart and compare it to real data"
  chromeTitle="You Draw It: What Got Better or Worse During Obama's Presidency"
  chromeUrl="https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2017/01/15/us/politics/you-draw-obama-legacy.html"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The Washington Post runs a [daily news quiz](https://www.washingtonpost.com/games/news-quiz/) where readers test their knowledge of the week's events. ProPublica built [Dollars for Docs](https://projects.propublica.org/docdollars/), a searchable database where you type in your doctor's name and see how much money they've received from pharmaceutical companies.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-4/propublica-dollars-for-docs.png"
