  chromeTitle="Dollars for Docs"
  chromeUrl="https://projects.propublica.org/docdollars/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

All of these projects share the same fundamental pattern: the reader provides input, the code processes it, and the page updates instantly. No reload. No waiting. The interface just responds.

Web developers have a name for this pattern. They call it [reactivity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reactive_programming). And Svelte — the framework we've been using — was specifically designed to make reactive interfaces easy to build. It provides a handful of simple tools that handle the hard parts for you: tracking what the user has done, figuring out what needs to change, and updating the page automatically.

Today we're going to learn those tools by building three interactive components, each one introducing new concepts that build on the last. By the end of class, you'll understand how to track state, respond to user actions, and compute values on the fly.

## Part 1: Show/Hide Toggle

Let's start with the most basic interaction: clicking a button to show or hide content.

Think about the interactive features you encounter on news websites every day. A "Read more" button that reveals additional methodology. A toggle that shows or hides a correction notice. An expandable section that lets the reader dig deeper without cluttering the page.

These are small features, but they all depend on the same concept: the page needs to remember whether something is open or closed, and update itself when the reader clicks.

### Setting up your project

Before we dive in, let's create a fresh project to work in. Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

Click the green "Use this template" button and create a new repository called `my-first-interactive-components`. Make sure "Public" is selected, then click "Create repository."

Clone it to your computer using the techniques from [week one](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-1). Open the project in Visual Studio Code, install the dependencies with `npm install`, and start the development server with `npm run dev`.

Now we're ready to build.

### Understanding state

In programming, "state" is a fancy word for information that can change over time. A toggle button has state: it's either on or off. A counter has state: it tracks the current number. A form has state: it holds whatever the user has typed.

In Svelte 5, we create state using a special function called `$state()`. This is one of Svelte's "runes"—magic keywords that tell Svelte to pay attention to a variable and update the page whenever it changes.

Here's the simplest possible example:

```js
let isVisible = $state(false);
```

This creates a variable called `isVisible` that starts as `false`. The `$state()` wrapper tells Svelte to track this variable. Whenever we change it, Svelte will automatically update any part of the page that uses it.

### Building the ReadMore component

Let's put this into practice. We'll build a component that shows a "Read more" button. When clicked, it reveals hidden content. Click again, and the content collapses.

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/ReadMore.svelte`. Start with the basic structure:

```svelte
<script>
  let { title, children } = $props();
  let isExpanded = $state(false);
</script>
```

We're accepting two props: a `title` that appears on the button, and `children` for whatever content should be hidden or revealed. We also create a state variable `isExpanded` that starts as `false`.

Now let's add the markup:

```svelte
<script>
  let { title, children } = $props();
  let isExpanded = $state(false);
</script>

<div class="read-more">
  <button onclick={() => isExpanded = !isExpanded}>
    {isExpanded ? '▼' : '▶'} {title}
  </button>

  {#if isExpanded}
    <div class="content">
      {@render children()}
    </div>
  {/if}
</div>
```

There are a few new things happening here. Let's break them down.

The `onclick` attribute is an event handler. It tells Svelte what to do when the user clicks the button. In this case, we're using an arrow function that flips the value of `isExpanded`. If it's `false`, it becomes `true`. If it's `true`, it becomes `false`. This is a common pattern called toggling.

The `{isExpanded ? '▼' : '▶'}` syntax is called a ternary operator. It's a shorthand way of saying "if `isExpanded` is true, show a down arrow; otherwise, show a right arrow." This gives the user a visual cue about whether the content is expanded or collapsed.

The `{#if isExpanded}` block is Svelte's conditional rendering syntax. Everything inside this block only appears on the page when `isExpanded` is `true`. When the user clicks the button and `isExpanded` becomes `true`, Svelte automatically adds this content to the page. When it becomes `false`, Svelte removes it.

Finally, let's add some styling. Copy and paste this into the bottom of the file:

```svelte
<script>
  let { title, children } = $props();
  let isExpanded = $state(false);
</script>

<div class="read-more">
  <button onclick={() => isExpanded = !isExpanded}>
    {isExpanded ? '▼' : '▶'} {title}
  </button>

  {#if isExpanded}
    <div class="content">
      {@render children()}
    </div>
  {/if}
</div>

<style>
  .read-more {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-light-gray);
  }

  button {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 1rem;
    text-align: left;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    border: none;
    font-weight: bold;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .content {
    padding: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-light-gray);
  }
</style>
```

### Using the component

Save your `ReadMore.svelte` file and open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Add an import at the top of the script block:

```js
import ReadMore from '$lib/components/ReadMore.svelte';
```

Now add the component somewhere in your page content, inside the `ArticleBody`:

```svelte
<ReadMore title="Methodology">
  <p>
    This analysis is based on data from the U.S. Census Bureau's
    American Community Survey, 2019-2023 5-year estimates. We calculated
    median household income by ZIP code and adjusted for inflation using
    the Bureau of Labor Statistics' Consumer Price Index.
  </p>
</ReadMore>
```

Save the file and check your browser at [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173). You should see a button labeled "Methodology" with a right arrow. Click it, and the methodology text appears. Click again, and it disappears.

Try adding a few more ReadMore components with different content:

```svelte
<ReadMore title="About the data">
  <p>
    The data for this project was obtained through a public records request
    filed with the city's Department of Finance on January 15, 2026.
  </p>
</ReadMore>

<ReadMore title="Correction">
  <p>
    An earlier version of this story incorrectly stated the mayor's
    proposed budget was $98 billion. It is $89 billion. The story has
    been updated.
  </p>
</ReadMore>
```

Notice how each ReadMore component maintains its own state. Clicking one doesn't affect the others. This is because each component instance has its own `isExpanded` variable. This independence is one of the powerful features of component-based architecture.

## Part 2: Streaming Survey

Now that you understand the basics of state and event handlers, let's build something that introduces a new concept: values that automatically calculate themselves from other values.

We're going to build an in-class survey. I'm going to ask how many of you subscribe to each of the major streaming services. You'll click buttons to record the votes, and the component will tally everything up in real time.

This is exactly the kind of interactive element that news organizations build for reader engagement. You've probably seen polls embedded in stories asking readers to weigh in on a topic. "Do you think the city should raise the minimum wage?" "Should the Olympics add esports?" These tools are simple to build, and they demonstrate a powerful Svelte concept.

### Starting with one service

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/StreamingSurvey.svelte`. Let's start small, with just one streaming service:

```svelte
<script>
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
</script>

<div class="survey">
  <h3>Streaming Survey</h3>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Netflix</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => netflixYes++}>
        Yes ({netflixYes})
      </button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => netflixNo++}>
        No ({netflixNo})
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

This should feel familiar from Part 1. We have two `$state` variables, one for yes votes and one for no votes. Each button increments its counter when clicked. The current count is displayed right on the button.

Copy and paste the styles I've provided below. We won't spend much time on the CSS today—our focus is on the JavaScript. But the green and red button colors help make the yes/no distinction clear at a glance.

```svelte
<style>
  .survey {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .service {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.75rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
  }

  .name {
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .buttons {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  button {
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    border: none;
    font-weight: bold;
    cursor: pointer;
    color: white;
  }

  .yes-btn {
    background: #2e7d32;
  }

  .no-btn {
    background: #c62828;
  }
</style>
```

Save the file, then open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and import it:

```js
import StreamingSurvey from '$lib/components/StreamingSurvey.svelte';
```

Place it in your page:

```svelte
<StreamingSurvey />
```

Check your browser. You should see Netflix with yes and no buttons. Click them and watch the counts update. It works, but it's just one service. Let's expand.

### Introducing $derived()

Before we add more services, let's introduce a new concept. What if we want to know the total number of votes for Netflix? We could write `{netflixYes + netflixNo}` in our markup, and it would work. But Svelte offers a better way: the `$derived()` rune.

```svelte
<script>
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
  let netflixTotal = $derived(netflixYes + netflixNo);
</script>
```

The `$derived()` rune creates a value that automatically recalculates whenever the values it depends on change. When `netflixYes` or `netflixNo` changes, `netflixTotal` automatically updates. You never have to manually recalculate it. Svelte handles the math for you.

We can derive more than just a sum. How about the percentage of yes votes?

```svelte
<script>
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
  let netflixTotal = $derived(netflixYes + netflixNo);
  let netflixPercent = $derived(
    netflixTotal > 0 ? Math.round((netflixYes / netflixTotal) * 100) : 0
  );
</script>
```

Notice the `netflixTotal > 0 ?` check. This prevents a division-by-zero error when no votes have been cast. If there are no votes, we just show 0%. Otherwise, we calculate the percentage and round it to a whole number with `Math.round()`.

Let's add this to our display. Update the markup to show the total and percentage below the buttons:

```svelte
<div class="service">
  <span class="name">Netflix</span>
  <div class="buttons">
    <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => netflixYes++}>
      Yes ({netflixYes})
    </button>
    <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => netflixNo++}>
      No ({netflixNo})
    </button>
  </div>
  <span class="percent">{netflixPercent}%</span>
</div>
```

Add a style for the percentage to your `<style>` block:

```css
.percent {
  font-weight: bold;
  min-width: 50px;
  text-align: right;
}
```

Save and test. As you click yes and no, the percentage updates instantly. This is `$derived()` in action—you change the inputs, and every value that depends on them recalculates automatically.

### Scaling up to multiple services

Now let's add the rest of our streaming services. Update the script block with state and derived values for each one:

```svelte
<script>
  // Netflix
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
  let netflixTotal = $derived(netflixYes + netflixNo);
  let netflixPercent = $derived(
    netflixTotal > 0 ? Math.round((netflixYes / netflixTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Hulu
  let huluYes = $state(0);
  let huluNo = $state(0);
  let huluTotal = $derived(huluYes + huluNo);
  let huluPercent = $derived(
    huluTotal > 0 ? Math.round((huluYes / huluTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Disney+
  let disneyYes = $state(0);
  let disneyNo = $state(0);
  let disneyTotal = $derived(disneyYes + disneyNo);
  let disneyPercent = $derived(
    disneyTotal > 0 ? Math.round((disneyYes / disneyTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Max
  let maxYes = $state(0);
  let maxNo = $state(0);
  let maxTotal = $derived(maxYes + maxNo);
  let maxPercent = $derived(
    maxTotal > 0 ? Math.round((maxYes / maxTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Apple TV+
  let appleYes = $state(0);
  let appleNo = $state(0);
  let appleTotal = $derived(appleYes + appleNo);
  let applePercent = $derived(
    appleTotal > 0 ? Math.round((appleYes / appleTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Totals across all services
  let totalYes = $derived(netflixYes + huluYes + disneyYes + maxYes + appleYes);
  let totalNo = $derived(netflixNo + huluNo + disneyNo + maxNo + appleNo);
  let totalVotes = $derived(totalYes + totalNo);
  let overallPercent = $derived(
    totalVotes > 0 ? Math.round((totalYes / totalVotes) * 100) : 0
  );
</script>
```

Yes, that's a lot of variables. You might be thinking there has to be a better way to organize this, and you'd be right. Later in the course, when we work with data structures like arrays and objects, we'll learn how to handle repetitive patterns more elegantly. For now, the repetition is actually useful, because it makes the pattern crystal clear: state in, derived values out.

Look at those last four lines. `totalYes` and `totalNo` derive from all five services. `totalVotes` derives from `totalYes` and `totalNo`. And `overallPercent` derives from `totalYes` and `totalVotes`. This is a chain of derived values—each one building on the ones before it. Svelte keeps every link in the chain up to date automatically.

Now update the markup with all five services and a summary footer:

```svelte
<div class="survey">
  <h3>📺 Do you subscribe to...</h3>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Netflix</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => netflixYes++}>Yes ({netflixYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => netflixNo++}>No ({netflixNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{netflixPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Hulu</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => huluYes++}>Yes ({huluYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => huluNo++}>No ({huluNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{huluPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Disney+</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => disneyYes++}>Yes ({disneyYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => disneyNo++}>No ({disneyNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{disneyPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Max</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => maxYes++}>Yes ({maxYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => maxNo++}>No ({maxNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{maxPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Apple TV+</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => appleYes++}>Yes ({appleYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => appleNo++}>No ({appleNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{applePercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="summary">
    <div class="summary-stat">
      <span class="summary-number">{totalVotes}</span>
      <span class="summary-label">Total Votes</span>
    </div>
    <div class="summary-stat">
      <span class="summary-number">{totalYes}</span>
      <span class="summary-label">Subscriptions</span>
    </div>
    <div class="summary-stat">
      <span class="summary-number">{overallPercent}%</span>
      <span class="summary-label">Subscribe Rate</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

Add the summary styles to your `<style>` block:

```css
.summary {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-around;
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding-top: 1rem;
  border-top: 2px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
}

.summary-stat {
  text-align: center;
}

.summary-number {
  display: block;
  font-size: 2rem;
  font-weight: bold;
}

.summary-label {
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}
```

Save and check your browser. You should see all five streaming services with yes and no buttons, individual percentages, and a summary row at the bottom that tallies everything up.

Let's try it. I'll poll the room. Raise your hand if you subscribe to Netflix. Count the hands, click yes that many times. Now the rest of you, click no. Move on to Hulu. Disney+. Max. Apple TV+.

Watch the summary row as we go. The total votes climb. The subscription count rises. The overall percentage adjusts. Every single number on the page is either a `$state` that we're clicking to change, or a `$derived` value that recalculates automatically. Nothing is stale. Nothing is out of sync.

### Why use $derived()?

You might wonder why we need `$derived()` when we could just write `{netflixYes + netflixNo}` directly in the template. There are a few reasons:

1. **Organization**: Keeping calculations in the script block makes your template cleaner and your logic easier to find.

2. **Reuse**: If you need to use the total in multiple places, you only define it once.

3. **Complexity**: For more complex calculations, `$derived()` keeps things readable. Imagine a formula like `$derived(netflixTotal > 0 ? Math.round((netflixYes / netflixTotal) * 100) : 0)` for a percentage—that would clutter your template.

4. **Chaining**: Derived values can depend on other derived values. Our `overallPercent` depends on `totalYes` and `totalVotes`, which themselves depend on the individual service counts. Svelte sorts out the dependency chain for you.

<Aside title="The rising cost of streaming">
  <p>If you're curious about how much all these subscriptions add up to, the numbers are staggering. As of early 2026, Netflix's ad-free plan costs $17.99 a month. Disney+ is $11.99 with ads. Max is $10.99. Apple TV+ is $12.99. Hulu is $9.99. That's over $63 a month just for five services—and it doesn't include the ad-free tiers, which can push the total well past $90. According to a 2025 survey by Reviews.org, U.S. consumers spent an average of $278 per month to stay connected to their chosen streaming platforms. News organizations cover this "streamflation" beat regularly, often with exactly the kind of interactive calculators we're learning to build.</p>
</Aside>

## Part 3: Tip Calculator

For our final component, we're going to build something more ambitious: a tip calculator that responds to form inputs. This will introduce one of Svelte's most powerful features: two-way data binding with `bind:value`.

News organizations love calculators. They let readers personalize a story by entering their own information. "How much would you save under the new tax plan?" "What's your risk level based on these factors?" "How does your commute compare to the city average?"

These tools increase engagement because they make the story about the reader. And with Svelte, they're surprisingly easy to build.

### Understanding two-way binding

So far, we've seen one-way interactions: the user clicks a button, and something changes. But form inputs work differently. A text field or slider needs to both display a value and accept changes to that value.

Svelte handles this with `bind:value`, which creates a two-way connection between an input element and a state variable. When the variable changes, the input updates. When the user types or drags, the variable updates. Everything stays in sync automatically.

Here's a simple example:

```svelte
<script>
  let name = $state('');
</script>

<input type="text" bind:value={name}>
<p>Hello, {name}!</p>
```

As the user types, the paragraph instantly updates to greet them by name. No event handlers needed—Svelte handles the connection for you.

### Building the calculator step by step

Let's build our tip calculator in stages, adding features as we go.

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/TipCalculator.svelte`. Start with the most basic version:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tip = $derived(billAmount * 0.20);
</script>

<div class="calculator">
  <h3>Tip Calculator</h3>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label for="bill">Bill Amount</label>
    <div class="input-wrapper">
      <span class="currency">$</span>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="bill"
        bind:value={billAmount}
        min="0"
        step="0.01"
      >
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="result">
    <span class="label">20% Tip:</span>
    <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
</div>
```

The `bind:value={billAmount}` creates our two-way binding. The input displays the current value of `billAmount`, and typing a new number updates the variable.

The `tip` variable uses `$derived()` to calculate 20% of the bill. Notice the `.toFixed(2)` at the end—this formats the number to always show two decimal places, which is what we expect for currency.

Add some basic styling. Again, just copy and paste this—we'll focus more on CSS in later weeks:

```svelte
<style>
  .calculator {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    margin: 2rem 0;
    max-width: 400px;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .input-group {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  label {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .input-wrapper {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    border: 2px solid #ddd;
  }

  .currency {
    padding: 0.75rem;
    background: #eee;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  input[type="number"] {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    border: none;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    width: 100%;
  }

  .result {
    padding: 1rem;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: white;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .result .label {
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .result .value {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }
</style>
```

Import it into your page and try it out:

```js
import TipCalculator from '$lib/components/TipCalculator.svelte';
```

```svelte
<TipCalculator />
```

Type a bill amount and watch the tip calculate instantly. But 20% isn't always the right tip. Let's add a way to customize the percentage.

### Adding a custom tip percentage

Update your script to include a tip percentage variable:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
</script>
```

Now our `tip` calculation uses the `tipPercent` variable instead of a hardcoded 0.20. We start at 18% because that's a common default.

Add a range slider to let users adjust the percentage. Insert this after the bill amount input:

```svelte
<div class="input-group">
  <label for="percent">Tip: {tipPercent}%</label>
  <input
    type="range"
    id="percent"
    bind:value={tipPercent}
    min="10"
    max="30"
    step="1"
  >
</div>
```

The range input creates a slider. `bind:value={tipPercent}` connects it to our state. As the user drags, `tipPercent` updates, which triggers `tip` to recalculate, which updates the display. The label shows the current percentage using `{tipPercent}%`.

Update your result display to show the dynamic percentage:

```svelte
<div class="result">
  <span class="label">{tipPercent}% Tip:</span>
  <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
</div>
```

Add styling for the slider:

```css
input[type="range"] {
  width: 100%;
}
```

### Adding the total

Let's show the total bill including tip:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
  let total = $derived(billAmount + tip);
</script>
```

Add another result display:

```svelte
<div class="results">
  <div class="result">
    <span class="label">{tipPercent}% Tip:</span>
    <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
  <div class="result total">
    <span class="label">Total:</span>
    <span class="value">${total.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
</div>
```

Update the styles to handle multiple results:

```css
.results {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  margin-top: 1rem;
}

.result.total {
  background: var(--color-dark-gray);
}
```

### Splitting the bill

For the final feature, let's add the ability to split the bill among multiple people:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let numPeople = $state(1);

  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
  let total = $derived(billAmount + tip);
  let perPerson = $derived(total / numPeople);
</script>
```

Add an input for the number of people, after the tip slider:

```svelte
<div class="input-group">
  <label>Split between</label>
  <div class="people-input">
    <button onclick={() => numPeople = Math.max(1, numPeople - 1)}>−</button>
    <span class="people-count">{numPeople}</span>
    <button onclick={() => numPeople++}>+</button>
  </div>
</div>
```

Notice we use `Math.max(1, numPeople - 1)` for the minus button. This ensures we never go below 1 person—you can't split a bill zero ways!

Add the per-person result:

```svelte
{#if numPeople > 1}
  <div class="result per-person">
    <span class="label">Per Person:</span>
    <span class="value">${perPerson.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
{/if}
```

The `{#if numPeople > 1}` only shows this line when splitting among multiple people. No need to show "per person" when there's only one person.

Here's the styling for the people input and the per-person result:

```css
.people-input {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1rem;
}

.people-input button {
  width: 40px;
  height: 40px;
  border: none;
  background: var(--color-accent);
  color: white;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.people-count {
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  font-weight: bold;
}

.result.per-person {
  background: #1565c0;
}
```

### The complete component

Here's the final `TipCalculator.svelte` with all features:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let numPeople = $state(1);

  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
  let total = $derived(billAmount + tip);
  let perPerson = $derived(total / numPeople);
</script>

<div class="calculator">
  <h3>Tip Calculator</h3>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label for="bill">Bill Amount</label>
    <div class="input-wrapper">
      <span class="currency">$</span>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="bill"
        bind:value={billAmount}
        min="0"
        step="0.01"
      >
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label for="percent">Tip: {tipPercent}%</label>
    <input
      type="range"
      id="percent"
      bind:value={tipPercent}
      min="10"
      max="30"
      step="1"
    >
  </div>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label>Split between</label>
    <div class="people-input">
      <button onclick={() => numPeople = Math.max(1, numPeople - 1)}>−</button>
      <span class="people-count">{numPeople}</span>
      <button onclick={() => numPeople++}>+</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="results">
    <div class="result">
      <span class="label">{tipPercent}% Tip:</span>
      <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="result total">
      <span class="label">Total:</span>
      <span class="value">${total.toFixed(2)}</span>
    </div>
    {#if numPeople > 1}
      <div class="result per-person">
        <span class="label">Per Person:</span>
        <span class="value">${perPerson.toFixed(2)}</span>
      </div>
    {/if}
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .calculator {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    margin: 2rem 0;
    max-width: 400px;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .input-group {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  label {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .input-wrapper {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    border: 2px solid #ddd;
  }

  .currency {
    padding: 0.75rem;
    background: #eee;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  input[type="number"] {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    border: none;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    width: 100%;
  }

  input[type="range"] {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .people-input {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .people-input button {
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
    border: none;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: white;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .people-count {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .results {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .result {
    padding: 1rem;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: white;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .result .label {
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .result .value {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .result.total {
    background: var(--color-dark-gray);
  }

  .result.per-person {
    background: #1565c0;
  }
</style>
```

## What you've learned

Today we covered the core concepts of reactive programming in Svelte:

**State with `$state()`**: Create variables that Svelte tracks and responds to when they change. Use it for booleans (toggles), numbers (counters), strings (text input), or any value that needs to change.

**Event handlers with `onclick`**: Respond to user actions like button clicks. Use arrow functions to update state: `onclick={() => count++}`.

**Conditional rendering with `{#if}`**: Show or hide parts of your component based on state. Content inside an `{#if}` block only renders when the condition is true.

**Derived values with `$derived()`**: Create values that automatically recalculate when their dependencies change. Use for totals, percentages, formatting, or any computation based on state.

**Two-way binding with `bind:value`**: Connect form inputs directly to state variables. Changes flow both ways—update the variable and the input updates; change the input and the variable updates.

These concepts are the foundation of interactive web development. With them, you can build calculators, quizzes, filters, visualizations, and countless other tools that engage your readers.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build three interactive components

Create a new repository from our class template and build three interactive components from scratch. Pick from this list or invent your own:

- **Time until election countdown**: Accept a date input and display days, hours, minutes until that date. Update automatically (hint: you'll need `setInterval`).

- **Rent split calculator**: Enter total rent and number of roommates. Show what each person owes. Bonus: allow unequal splits with percentage inputs.

- **Reading time estimator**: Paste text into a textarea. Calculate and display estimated reading time based on word count (assume 200 words per minute).

- **Quiz question**: Display a multiple choice question. When the user selects an answer, show whether they're right or wrong with an explanation.

- **Unit converter**: Convert between miles and kilometers, Fahrenheit and Celsius, or dollars and euros. Let users type in either field and see the other update.

Deploy your page to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

